---
title: Packing for the shoulder season
summary: The weeks either side of summer are the best time to travel and the hardest to pack for. A short method that fits in one bag, and what to leave at home.
minutes: 3
published: '2026-06-30'
---

The shoulder season — May and June, September and October in most of Europe and Japan — is the argument for travelling at all: the light is lower, the prices are lower, the queues are shorter. It is also the season in which the same afternoon can be twenty-six degrees and then, an hour later, not.

## The method

Pack by layer, not by day. Three thin layers that go together beat one thick one that is right for a single hour of the trip. In practice, for a week:

- Two pairs of trousers, one of which you would wear to dinner.
- Four tops that all go with both.
- One warm layer — a light merino jumper does more than a fleece and packs to nothing.
- One shell. Not a coat. A shell that folds into its own pocket and stops the rain that the forecast did not mention.
- Shoes you have already walked twenty kilometres in. This is the rule people break, and the one that ruins trips.

## What to leave

A second pair of shoes "for the evening". A travel pillow. Anything you might buy there for less than the cost of carrying it. The umbrella: every city sells them at the first drop, and every hotel lends them.

## The bag

One that goes under the seat if you are flying, one you can carry up four flights if you are not, because the old part of every city in this app has stairs and no lift. Weigh it, then take something out.

## Where the plan comes in

Put the forecast in the notes the night before each travel day and leave it there. A plan that says "rain in Porto on Thursday" beside the Thursday stops is the difference between a wet day and a museum day.
